A window is a component on bubbletea and android too: each overrides
`ui.window` with a `Screen` primitive of its own, and the one window a
terminal or an activity shows is the one node at the root of the package
body.

`visible` is the window's two-way prop there as on the desktop hosts. The
close is the host's: ctrl+c on bubbletea, the system back at the root on
android. It is reported as `visible = false`, then `@closed` runs, and the
program ends only when the window is still off screen after that -- so the
handler here keeps it open twice by calling `main.open()`, which writes
`shown`, the var `:visible` binds. `main.close()` takes it off screen without
ending anything: a hidden window renders nothing.

deny out/go/bubbletea/model.go `case msg\.Code == 'c' && msg\.Mod == tea\.ModCtrl:\n\s*return m, tea\.Quit` -- ctrl+c is the window's close, reported before anything quits
deny * `\.open\(\)|\.close\(\)` -- open and close are the window's methods, spliced where they are called

-- app.sngl --
import ui "sngl:ui"

output {
    go {
        bubbletea
    }
    kotlin {
        android
    }
}

var (
    shown = true
    closes = 0
)

ui.window #main(title="Main", :visible=shown, @closed {
    closes += 1
    if closes < 3 {
        main.open()
    }
}) {
    ui.text(value="closed {closes} times")
    ui.button(text="Hide", @click {
        main.close()
    })
}
